115
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:15,940
 And I think what I learned in that role was that there was a lot of playtime, a lot of proof of concepts,

116
00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:21,000
 a lot of side of desks initiatives and people's pet projects.

117
00:07:21,220 --> 00:07:24,360
 But very few of those initiatives actually made it into production.

118
00:07:25,220 --> 00:07:30,040
 And when I looked at it, and when I spoke to people in the organization, what I found was a lot of people,

119
00:07:30,760 --> 00:07:36,940
 when you asked them about the success rate that they thought we would get as a firm from proof of concepts to production,

120
00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:42,160
 you know, they were saying things like, you know, half of the POCs we do are going to make it into live.

121
00:07:42,700 --> 00:07:48,000
 When you asked them what they thought the pilot success rate into production was, it was more like 90%.

122
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:53,960
 But when we actually looked for the data, the truth was that only about 10% of proof of concepts ever made into production,

123
00:07:54,140 --> 00:07:57,080
 only about 50% sort of pilots did.

124
00:07:57,560 --> 00:08:02,020
 And I think that was a really interesting exercise to go through,

125
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:08,300
 because what we did was we started to look at the value in terms of the impact of the organization

126
00:08:08,300 --> 00:08:11,100
 of each of the initiatives that we were running.

127
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:12,820
 And then we essentially discounted it.

128
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:15,640
 We multiplied it by the likelihood of it making production.

129
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:22,140
 And then with a metric that we then held in our AI center was the total value of that portfolio.

130
00:08:22,660 --> 00:08:26,600
 And what that meant was that I could go to the CEO or the board and say, you know,

131
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:32,380
 my budget is X and the value of my portfolio, hopefully, was always a multiple of X.

132
00:08:32,380 --> 00:08:34,760
 And therefore, you should give me more money so I can do more AI.

133
00:08:35,480 --> 00:08:37,000
 But it was a very, very deliberate strategy.

134
00:08:37,660 --> 00:08:44,160
 And by doing that, we were much, much better able to be selective as to which vendors to work with,

135
00:08:44,300 --> 00:08:49,020
 which initiatives to pursue, and also the right level of governance around each opportunity.

136
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:53,060
 So that thinking, we've definitely applied now with the other side of the table.

137
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:54,480
 We're selling it to organizations.

138
00:08:55,340 --> 00:08:59,820
 So we don't do proof of concepts when, you know, really we don't think there's any chance

139
00:08:59,820 --> 00:09:00,800
 of it making into production.

140
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:05,780
 Pilots are things we make sure all the controls are in place and all of the things

141
